
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false, "If the return value is false, last position of x in new list is exactly -1");
</code>
After running these codes in your debug environment, you would typically observe the failure of the Debug Assert when the condition in New_Ret is false. The expression New_Ret == false checks whether the return value is false since we are expecting the absence of an occurrence of x in the array list which should occur when New_Ret is false. The string "If the return value is false, last position of x in new list is exactly -1" is used as a message for the Debug Assert.--------------------
@Debug.Assert((New_Ret && Old_arrListCount + 1 == New_arrListCount && Old_arrListIndexOfX + 1 == New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1 == New_arrListLastIndexOfX) || (!New_Ret && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount && Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX))
</code>

This code does the following:
If the list containing x does not exist, the list count increases by 1 and all indexes are modified accordingly.
If the list does exist, the list count remains the same and all indexes remain the same.
If this condition does not hold true, the assert statement will fail.--------------------
Assert.True(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);
</code>

